# Line-ending policy.
#
# Why this file exists: `core.autocrlf = true` on the Windows dev machines, and there was no
# .gitattributes, so git rewrites the WORKING TREE to CRLF on checkout.
#
# Measured 2026-10-02 (`git ls-files --eol | awk '{print $1,$2}' | sort | uniq -c`):
#     107  i/lf w/crlf     <- the worktree is already being rewritten, repo-wide
#      32  i/lf w/lf
#       7  i/-text w/-text  (binary)
#
# For TypeScript, Markdown and JSON that is cosmetic - git normalises on comparison, so the files
# still read as clean. For an EXECUTABLE SCRIPT it is a defect:
#
#   * a shebang becomes `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` -> "./script.sh: bad interpreter"
#   * a CR inside a `case`, heredoc or `[[ ]]` construct -> "$'\r': command not found"
#
# So the policy is deliberately narrow: pin the file types that BREAK, and leave the rest of the
# repository's behaviour alone. If you add a new executable script language, add it here too.
#
# Repo-wide normalisation (`* text=auto eol=lf`) is a separate, larger decision - it would touch
# the worktree of every file. Not taken here.

# Shell and Python scripts must keep LF endings.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf

# Keep this file itself stable.
.gitattributes text eol=lf
